-- title:
--   Diamond operators descend to both special-fibre components
-- statement:
--   The setting is the two-chart integral model of $X_1(Mp)$ over a discrete valuation ring and the two components of its special fibre in characteristic $p$.
--
--   **Arithmetic data.** A prime $p$; an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$; a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, together with a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$; an intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L((q))$ subject to `hK`, namely $K =$ `laurentBaseChange L (x1FunctionField (M*p))`, the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; a discrete valuation domain $A$ with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$; and an element $j \in K$, nonzero, whose Laurent expansion is `coeffEmb L jq`, the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion of the modular function $j$ (`hj`). The scheme under consideration is [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), assumed proper over $\operatorname{Spec} A$.
--
--   **Special fibre.** An algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, an $A$-algebra; two proper smooth geometrically integral curves $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ of relative dimension $1$; closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ of $C_1$, $C_2$ into the base change of the model to $k$, over $k$; the hypothesis `hcover`, that every point of the fibre product of the model with $\operatorname{Spec} k$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; the hypothesis `hred`, that the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced; and a natural number $n > 0$ equal to the cardinality of that intersection (`hn`, `hn0`).
--
--   **Sections.** A section $\varepsilon$ of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, sections $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ of $c_1, c_2$, and the compatibility `hε₁`: $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base-changed section `sectionBaseChange k ε`.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** A `RelativePic0Designation` $D$ for the model over $A$ — a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section — together with `hrep`: $D$ represents, via a Poincaré rigidified line bundle, the subfunctor of the relative Picard functor cut out by the fibrewise algebraically trivial condition `algEquivZeroCut`; and the hypotheses `hsm`, `hsep` that $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth and separated. Further, `hreps`: the base change $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ k$ represents the corresponding condition for the curve over $k$ with section `sectionBaseChange k ε`; `hPk`: the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` is isomorphic to the base change along $k$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep` along the first projection; and designations $D_1$, $D_2$ for $c_1$, $c_2$ with representability data `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` relative to $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$.
--
--   **The two restriction maps.** The first is `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some`, the morphism $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ k).\mathrm{toBase} \to D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $k$ classifying pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $i_1$. The second is a given morphism $\nu_2$ over $k$ from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$, characterised by `hν₂`: for every scheme $T$ over $k$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ k).\mathrm{toBase}$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along the section `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` of the pullback along `curveChange i₂.1` of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   **Generic fibre and geometric model.** Algebra structures making $A \to L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ a tower; `hsmL`, `hgiL`: the base change of the model to $L$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral; `hprL`, `hgcL`: the base change of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $L$ is proper and geometrically connected. A `CurveModel` $M\eta$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the function field `x1FunctionFieldBar (M*p)` (a proper smooth integral curve with a distinguished isomorphism of its function field with that field and a bijection from its closed points to the places), an isomorphism $e\eta$ of $M\eta.C$ with the geometric fibre of the model compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`), the nonemptiness of the preimage of the finite chart under $e\eta$ followed by the first projection, and `hMηpin`: for every element $a$ of `chartAlgFin A K j`, the function-field element obtained by transporting $a$ through that chart has Laurent expansion equal to the coefficientwise image along $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the expansion of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` states that for every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and every two geometric points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$ with $x'$ followed by the chart morphism equal to $\operatorname{Spec} g$ followed by $x$ followed by the chart morphism, the place attached to $x'$ is the image of the place attached to $x$ under the semilinear automorphism `arithmeticGalois (x1FunctionField (M*p)) g`.
--
--   **Hecke and Galois data.** `hin`: the package `HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M*p)`, namely Hecke input data at every prime together with, for every $d$ coprime to $M p$, a diamond automorphism of the function field over $\mathbb{Q}$ and a base-changed counterpart over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hcomm`: the generators `heckeDiamondGenBar (M*p)` commute pairwise. A multiplicative semiring action of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ with `hΓA`: the structure map $A \to L$ is equivariant. An equivalence `gpts` between $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the geometric function field, `JOne (M*p)`, and the sections of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; a family $\varphi$ of endomorphisms of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ indexed by `HeckeAlgOne`; and a family $\tau$ assigning to each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ a morphism $D.P \to D.P$ over the $s$-twisted structure morphism. These satisfy `hφmul` (each $\varphi(t)$ is additive for the relative group law of `hrep.some`), `hφpts` (for the module structure `heckeModuleOneBar (M*p)`, $\mathrm{gpts}(t \cdot x) = \mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\varphi(t)$), `hτ1` and `hτmul` ($\tau(1)$ is the identity and $\tau(ss')$ is $\tau(s)$ followed by $\tau(s')$), `hτφ` ($\tau(s)$ and $\varphi(t)$ commute), `hgadd` (`gpts` is additive for the relative group law), and `hτpts` (for $\sigma'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ restricting to $s$ on $L$, $\mathrm{gpts}(\sigma' \cdot x)$ is $\operatorname{Spec}\sigma'$ followed by $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$)).
--
--   **Special-fibre group data.** A structure `G : ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`, consisting of abelian groups $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$, a subgroup `G.torus` of $G.J_{0s}$, and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J_{0s} \to G.J_I \times G.J_E$ with kernel `G.torus`; equivalences `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` of these three groups with the $k$-points of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ k).\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$; the additivity hypotheses `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE`, each stating that the pullback of the relevant Poincaré bundle along the point attached to a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks along the points attached to the summands; and `hproj`, stating that for every $x \in G.J_{0s}$ the point $\mathrm{ptsI}((G.\mathrm{proj}\,x)_1)$ is $\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by the first restriction map, and $\mathrm{ptsE}((G.\mathrm{proj}\,x)_2)$ is $\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi data.** Representability `hDL` of $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ L$ over $L$; a morphism $\mathrm{ajL}$ over $L$ from the base-changed curve to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ L).\mathrm{toBase}$; a morphism $kL$ from the geometric fibre to the $L$-fibre of the model with `hkL₁`, `hkL₂` expressing compatibility with both projections; a morphism $\overline{\mathrm{aj}} : M\eta.C \to D.P$; a geometric point $\overline{\varepsilon}$ of $M\eta.C$; `hPL`, the comparison of the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` with the base change along $L$ of the pullback of that of `hrep`; `hajLε`, that the section followed by $\mathrm{ajL}$ is the zero section; `hajL`, the Abel–Jacobi property: for every field $K'$, every morphism $t : \operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of the base-changed curve, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajL}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the section, that is to $\mathcal{O}(x - \varepsilon)$; `hajbar`, that $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ is the composite of $e\eta$, $kL$, $\mathrm{ajL}$ and the first projection, together with `hajbar_over` expressing that it lies over $M\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$; `hεbar` and `hεbar_aj`, that $\overline{\varepsilon}$ lies over $\varepsilon$ in the chart and is carried by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ to the zero section; and `hpts_aj`, that for all geometric points $x, s$ of $M\eta.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$ there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ on the geometric function field equal to $[\,\text{place of } x\,] - [\,\text{place of } s\,]$ with $\mathrm{gpts}(\,[Dv]\,)$ equal to $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every natural number $d$ coprime to $Mp$ there exist a morphism $u_1 : D_1.\mathrm{toBase} \to D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ and a morphism $u_2 : D_2.\mathrm{toBase} \to D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ such that:
--
--   (i) for every commutative ring $T'$ that is a $k$-algebra and all pairs $v, v'$ of $T'$-points of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ k).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $k \to T'$, if $v'$ followed by the projection $\mathrm{pr}_1 : D.\mathrm{baseChange}\ k \to D.P$ equals $v$ followed by $\mathrm{pr}_1$ followed by $\varphi(\langle d\rangle)$, where $\langle d\rangle$ denotes [`ModularCurve.diamondGen d`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L20), then both $v'$ followed by the first restriction map equals $v$ followed by the first restriction map followed by $u_1$, and $v'$ followed by $\nu_2$ equals $v$ followed by $\nu_2$ followed by $u_2$;
--
--   (ii) $u_1$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law of $D_1$ determined by `hrep₁.some` and `algEquivZeroGroupCut`: for every scheme $T$ over $k$ and all $T$-points $x, y$ of $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$, the product $x \cdot y$ followed by $u_1$ equals the product of $x$ followed by $u_1$ and $y$ followed by $u_1$;
--
--   (iii) likewise $u_2$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law of $D_2$ determined by `hrep₂.some`.
--
--   No uniqueness of $u_1$, $u_2$ is asserted.
--
--   This is the diagonal descent of the diamond operator $\langle d\rangle$, for $d$ coprime to $Mp$, from the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ to the two components of the special fibre at $p$, in the form of endomorphisms $u_1$, $u_2$ of the component Picard schemes compatible with the two restriction maps and with the relative group laws. It feeds the assembly of the special-fibre operator package for $J_1(Mp)$ in characteristic $p$ and the computations of the images of diamond operators on the pair of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$'s used in the level-lowering argument.
